{
  "image_name": "2a35e9aa09192075fe36db494236e6fc.png",
  "prompt_text": "This figure summarizes how IL-9 contributes to type 2 allergic inflammation. On the left, a naïve CD4+ T cell is shown differentiating into both TH2 and TH9 cells after antigen presentation, with TGFβ plus IL-4 indicated as the main cytokine drivers and IL-25, IL-33, and TSLP as amplifiers. TH9 cells mainly produce IL-9, while TH2 cells produce IL-9 together with other type 2 cytokines such as IL-4, IL-5, and IL-13.\n\nThe center of the figure shows the downstream targets of these signals. IL-9 and related type 2 responses promote mast cell activation and mediator release, B-cell memory responses and IgE production, epithelial mucus and chemokine secretion, and eosinophil-driven inflammation. Together these effects build a coordinated allergic effector program.\n\nOn the right, innate cells appear to reinforce this network. ILC2 cells, stimulated by IL-25, IL-33, and TSLP, produce type 2 cytokines and seem to interact with IL-9 in a positive-feedback manner. Mucosal mast cells are also shown responding to IL-33 and IL-9 and producing IL-9/IL-13, suggesting local amplification.\n\nOverall, the figure presents IL-9 as a central amplifier linking adaptive and innate immunity to chronic allergic airway disease, IgE-mediated food allergy, and allergic skin inflammation.",
